1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:01,173
Lesson three.

2
00:00:01,173 --> 00:00:04,541
The most important skill with AI is checking.

3
00:00:04,541 --> 00:00:07,759
Even the best tools sometimes invent facts.

4
00:00:07,759 --> 00:00:11,800
Let us see how to catch mistakes before they cost you.

5
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:19,813
When an AI makes up a fact, a name, a number or a source, people call it a hallucination.

6
00:00:19,813 --> 00:00:23,747
It sounds confident, so it is easy to believe.

7
00:00:23,747 --> 00:00:25,800
You must learn to check.

8
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:27,643
Watch for red flags.

9
00:00:27,643 --> 00:00:30,241
Very exact numbers without a source.

10
00:00:30,241 --> 00:00:32,839
Links or books that you cannot find.

11
00:00:32,839 --> 00:00:35,798
Perfect answers about very recent events.

12
00:00:35,798 --> 00:00:40,200
And answers that change when you ask the same question again.

13
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:42,293
Use a three step check.

14
00:00:42,293 --> 00:00:43,766
Ask for the sources.

15
00:00:43,766 --> 00:00:47,815
Open them yourself, because the tool can invent a link.

16
00:00:47,815 --> 00:00:52,600
Then compare with a second, independent source before you decide.

17
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:00,189
Prefer official websites and documentation, original reports and data, and experts who sign their name.

18
00:01:00,189 --> 00:01:03,400
A random post on social media is not a source.

19
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:12,527
Be extra careful with money, health and legal topics, with prices and predictions, and with news from the last few days.

20
00:01:12,527 --> 00:01:17,400
In these cases, ask a real professional or use the official source.

21
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:19,179
Trust, but verify.

22
00:01:19,179 --> 00:01:22,779
This habit will protect you more than any tool.

23
00:01:22,779 --> 00:01:28,600
Next lesson: how to protect your privacy and your accounts while you use AI.

